course: biology
lesson: 61
title: Carnivorous and strange plants
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Most plants live quietly on light, air, water and a little from the soil, but some grow in wet, boggy ground so poor that they have turned into hunters of insects.
explain: Watch a Venus flytrap at the garden centre: a fly walks across the pink inside of the trap, brushes one tiny trigger hair, then another, and in about a tenth of a second the two halves slam shut like a mouth with green teeth.
explain: These plants still make their food from sunlight like any green plant; the insects are their «vitamins», giving them the nitrogen and minerals the bog cannot.
explain: Other plants are strange in different ways: a flower a metre wide with no leaves at all, a flower spike taller than a grown-up that stinks of rotting meat, a desert plant with just two leaves for hundreds of years, and a nettle that injects you with its sting.
explain: Plants do move, but slowly or with clever tricks, and no plant anywhere can eat a person, whatever cartoons say.
fact: A Venus flytrap shuts in about a tenth of a second when its trigger hairs are touched twice within about 20 seconds; one touch, like a raindrop, is not enough.
fact: After it closes, the flytrap seals tight and makes digestive juices; digesting one fly takes about a week to ten days, and then the trap opens again; if it closes on something it cannot digest, like a finger, it opens again in a day or so.
fact: Each flytrap can only close a few times before it dies off, so closing on nothing wastes its energy, and food that is too big, like a lump of meat, makes the trap rot.
fact: Wild Venus flytraps grow only in boggy places in North and South Carolina in the USA, where the soil has very little nitrogen.
fact: Pitcher plants have jug-shaped leaves with slippery rims and a pool of digestive liquid; insects come for nectar, slip in and drown.
fact: Some big pitcher plants in Borneo feed partly on the droppings of tree shrews, which lick sweet nectar from the lid and use the pitcher as a toilet.
fact: A sundew's leaves have red hairs tipped with sticky drops that glitter like dew; a stuck insect is slowly wrapped as the hairs bend over it.
fact: Bladderworts have no roots and float in ponds; their tiny underwater bags pop open and suck in water fleas in less than a thousandth of a second.
fact: The sensitive plant, Mimosa pudica, folds its leaflets within seconds when touched because water quickly moves out of cells at the base of the leaf; it opens again after several minutes.
fact: Rafflesia arnoldii from Sumatra and Borneo has the biggest single flower, about 1 metre across, and it has no leaves, stem or roots of its own: it lives as a parasite inside a vine.
fact: The titan arum, or corpse flower, from Sumatra sends up a flower spike up to about 3 metres tall that opens for only a day or two and may not bloom again for years; it warms itself and smells of rotting meat to call carrion beetles and flies.
fact: Welwitschia from the Namib Desert grows only two leaves in its whole life; they keep growing from the base and are torn into ribbons by the wind, and some plants are thought to be over 1,000 years old.
fact: A stinging nettle's hairs have glass-like tips that snap off and inject an itchy mix of chemicals into the skin; rubbing a dock leaf on the sting mostly just cools and distracts.
fact: Young sunflowers follow the Sun across the sky each day and turn back east at night, because the stem grows faster on one side; grown-up flower heads stop moving and mostly face east.
fact: No plant can eat a person; the largest meat-eating plants catch insects and, very rarely, a small frog, lizard or mouse, and the old story of a man-eating tree in Madagascar was a made-up newspaper tale.
exercise: How fast does a Venus flytrap shut? | In about a tenth of a second | In about ten minutes or a bit more | In about one hour, like a slow door | Slowly, over a whole day and night
exercise: What makes a Venus flytrap close? | Two touches of its trigger hairs within about 20 seconds | Any shadow at all passing over the open trap in daylight | One touch of any part of the leaf | The smell of a fly nearby
exercise: A raindrop hits one trigger hair of a flytrap. What happens? | Nothing, because one touch is not enough | The trap snaps shut at once and starts to digest | The trap opens wider to drink the raindrop | The trap turns black and dies off at once
exercise: Why do carnivorous plants catch insects? | To get nitrogen and minerals that their boggy soil lacks | Because they cannot make any food at all from sunlight like other plants | Because they have no water in their bog | To stop insects eating their flowers
exercise: Where do wild Venus flytraps grow? | Only in boggy places in North and South Carolina, USA | In hot rainforests and swamps all over Africa and India | In every garden in Britain | On high mountains in Asia
exercise: How long does a flytrap take to digest a fly? | About a week to ten days | About ten seconds, then it opens | About one hour or a little more | About a whole year or more
exercise: How does a pitcher plant catch insects? | They slip from its waxy rim into a pool of digestive liquid | It grabs them with long sticky green fingers that curl round them | It shoots a jet of water at them | It snaps shut like a pair of jaws
exercise: What do some Borneo pitcher plants get from tree shrews? | Their droppings, as food for the plant | Their soft fur, to line the inside of the pitcher | Their warmth, on cold nights | Their teeth, to cut up insects
exercise: How does a sundew trap an insect? | With sticky drops on its hairs, which then bend over it | With a trapdoor that sucks it underwater | With a slippery jug full of liquid that it cannot climb out of | With two halves that slam shut
exercise: How does a bladderwort catch water fleas? | Its tiny bags pop open and suck them in | It stings them with poison hairs | It wraps its long thin roots tightly around them | It lures them into a sticky flower
exercise: Why does a mimosa leaf fold up when touched? | Water quickly moves out of cells at the base of the leaf | Tiny muscles inside the leaf pull it shut, just like in your arm | The leaf is scared and hides from people | Wind from your hand blows it closed
exercise: What is strange about Rafflesia arnoldii? | It has a huge flower but no leaves, stem or roots | It grows only on the bottom of the sea | It has blue leaves and no flowers at all, ever | It flowers every single day for a hundred years
exercise: Why does a corpse flower smell of rotting meat? | To call carrion beetles and flies that carry its pollen | To scare away elephants that might eat it | Because it is rotting and dying | To catch and eat large animals that come close to sniff it
exercise: How tall can the flower spike of a titan arum grow? | Up to about 3 metres | About 30 centimetres at most | About as tall as a house of four floors | Only as big as a thumb
exercise: What is special about Welwitschia's leaves? | It grows only two leaves in its whole life | It grows a new set of leaves every single week | Its leaves are made of sharp spines | It has no leaves, only roots
exercise: How does a stinging nettle sting? | Hairs with glass-like tips snap and inject chemicals | Its leaves are covered in tiny teeth | It sprays acid from its roots | Insects living on it bite you when you brush against the leaves
exercise: Why does a young sunflower turn to follow the Sun? | Its stem grows faster on one side, so it bends | It has little eyes in the middle of the flower | Muscles in the stem pull it round | The wind always blows it round to face the Sun
exercise: Can any plant eat a person? | No, the biggest catch insects and rarely a frog or mouse | Yes, some trees in Madagascar do | Yes, but only the giant flytraps that grow deep in the jungle | Only if the person falls asleep near it
test: bro my flytrap closed on my finger, is it gonna eat me lol | No, it can't digest you, it will open again in a day or so | Yes, it will slowly digest your finger | Yes, you must pull it off fast or it will bite down even harder | No, but it will die of fright at once
test: Mia keeps poking her flytrap to watch it snap. Why is that bad for it? | Each trap can only close a few times, and closing empty wastes energy | It makes the trap grow far too big for the pot | It makes the plant poisonous | It teaches the trap to bite people
test: In a cartoon a giant jungle plant swallows the explorer whole. Put it right. | No plant can eat a person; the biggest catch insects and rarely more | That is true of the plants in the Amazon, they eat a few explorers a year | Only the corpse flower can do that | Real ones swallow only children
test: What would happen to a flytrap kept in a bog with no insects for a whole summer? | It would live on sunlight but grow weaker without the minerals | It would starve to death in a day | It would start eating the soil instead | It would slowly turn into a sundew so that it could catch flies another way
test: my nan says rub a dock leaf on a nettle sting and it's gone. does it work? | It mostly just cools the skin and takes your mind off it | Yes, the dock leaf pulls the sting out | Yes, the dock leaf is a proper medicine that cures all kinds of stings | No, it makes the sting twice as bad
test: Jake reckons sunflowers turn to the Sun all their lives, even when old. Right? | No, only young ones do; grown flower heads stop and mostly face east | Yes, they turn every day until they die | No, they never move at all | No, they turn away from the Sun to keep cool, all their lives, young or old
test: In a game, you feed your Venus flytrap a cheeseburger to make it grow fast. What would really happen? | The trap would likely rot, because it digests only small insects | It would grow huge and strong | It would turn into a pitcher plant | It would grow a big flower shaped like a burger, with a bun and a slice of cheese
test: Why can't bladderworts be seen catching prey on a pond walk? | Their traps are tiny and fire in under a thousandth of a second | They only hunt in the middle of winter | They catch fish only at night | They have no traps, only flowers
test: A queue at the botanic garden waits hours to sniff a titan arum. Why the rush? | It opens for only a day or two and may not bloom again for years | It opens every morning all year round | It smells of roses only on Sundays | It is only allowed to be seen once a year by law, on a special open day
test: ugh why does a flower wanna smell like dead meat?? that's gross | To call flies and beetles that visit dead animals to carry its pollen | Because it eats dead animals | To make people throw it away | Because it is already dead inside and rotting from the roots up, mate
test: A botanist says a Welwitschia in the Namib may be over 1,000 years old. What makes it so odd? | It has grown only the same two leaves all that time | It has grown a thousand new leaves every year | It has never needed any water at all | It has no leaves at all
exam: Identify the principal reason for which carnivorous plants capture animal prey. | To obtain nitrogen and minerals scarce in their boggy habitat | To obtain all the energy required for growth, sunlight being insufficient | To defend their flowers from insect damage | To supply themselves with water in drought
exam: It is given that a single trigger hair of a Venus flytrap is stimulated once only. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The trap ordinarily remains open | The trap closes at once with great force | The trap commences digestion forthwith | The trap is thereby permanently disabled
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises Rafflesia arnoldii? | A parasite bearing the largest single flower, without leaves, stem or roots | A carnivorous plant that captures small mammals | A desert plant possessing two perpetual leaves | A pond plant equipped with underwater bladders
exam: It is asserted that certain plants of tropical forests are capable of consuming a human being. Ascertain the correct position. | The assertion is false; the largest such plants seldom take prey above a mouse | The assertion is true of plants in Madagascar | The assertion is true of the titan arum | The assertion is true when the plant is mature
